WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.669
Film Courage: When did you decide you were
going to begin your own studio?

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:07.010
Jay Silverman, Photographer and Filmmaker:
When I graduated from Brooks Institute, I

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:09.419
think 1975 believe it or not?

00:00:09.419 --> 00:00:18.429
I was fortunate enough to occupy a rental
building that had a little teeny office and

00:00:18.429 --> 00:00:26.029
from the very beginning of my career I saw
the advantages of having some kind of exclusive

00:00:26.029 --> 00:00:30.270
place that I can go to each day rather than
work out of my house and that extended over

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:33.490
time and into me acquiring a studio.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:40.160
And then it became really a great part of
the complexion of my career is to have the

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:48.320
tools available 24/7 versus waking up in the
morning, producing a movie and renting everything.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:55.200
So I did what probably is not even possible
anymore but I started to acquire stuff and

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.829
save it and I did that through all of the
years that I’ve been in this business which

00:01:00.829 --> 00:01:03.100
has been quite a substantial amount of years.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.600
Film Courage: Like 37, you have on your [web]site?

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:11.000
Jay Silverman: I’ve been in the business
doing television and commercials for at least

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.150
20, but I’ve been in the field for 37 years.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:22.840
Film Courage: So going back 37 years what
did you envision success to look like versus

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.610
what do you envision it to be now?

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:28.680
Because I know when someone starts a venture
they have all these grand ideas about how

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.880
life’s going to be and what it’s going
to be like every day to run a business but

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:34.479
then having been in the trenches, 7 days a
week.

00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:41.780
Jay Silverman: Well, it’s a very interesting
question because as I reflect back on my career,

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:47.080
I’m one of the few guys that actually has
a left brain and a right brain, where I am

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.350
a businessman and I’m also a creative guy.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:56.031
And that’s an unusual combination and ultimately
I knew from the very beginning (and this is

00:01:56.031 --> 00:02:00.460
the same thing I tell my three daughters),
if you’re lucky enough to find a career

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:05.820
where you can do what you love, the money
and all those other things come later, but

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.960
they do come.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:14.530
I started from scratch and little by little
I built a business and one thing leads to

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:19.630
another and then finally (probably at the
beginning of the 1980’s) I started doing

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:24.650
what I call hybrid filmmaking which means
I would not only do still photography, I’d

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:31.530
do live action and it became a category that’s
now taken for granted because everybody wants

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:37.740
sound bites and everybody wants beautiful
visuals and now you can theoretically do pulls

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:44.070
off of a Red camera or the camera you are
using right now [Canon 5d Mark II].

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:49.280
And they are usually 2K or 4K and you don’t
even need to bring in a still photographer,

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.750
so I was doing both.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:57.760
Film Courage: What was the biggest surprise
for you in having run a business for over

00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:58.960
37 years, you know?

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:01.470
The 80’s were a much different time [financially]
than it is now.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:02.570
Social media wasn’t around.

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:05.420
Jay Silverman: The whole thing is about reinventing
yourself.

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:12.650
It’s funny, as I was driving into work in
the morning it became like almost an epiphany

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:18.280
in my own mind in that I’m not doing anything
like I was ten years ago.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:23.030
So I basically had to reinvent myself and
as I’ve looked back in my career, that’s

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:24.110
all that I’ve done.

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:26.910
Throughout my whole career is to reinvent
myself.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:31.800
You become a photographer (as what I was for
many years) and you all the sudden wake up

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:37.120
in the morning and go “My God I’ve been
working on this one client for four years.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:42.400
Inevitably that’s great for the pocket book,
not much for the advancements of what I want

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:43.820
to be doing creatively.”

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:45.520
So you adapt.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:51.870
And it’s crazy because I had one client
from ABC that told me “Why are you getting

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:54.980
that big studio in Hollywood.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:57.910
Can you actually make a living with that big
studio?”

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:03.980
And I know this is total cliche but it’s
like the Kevin Costner statement If You Build

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:08.890
It, They Will Come…I lucked out because
at the time that I built that studio (which

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:19.180
was at the end of 1999 I built this studio)
I found myself in a very unique situation

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:24.810
that I didn’t even realize which is most
people were getting rid of their studios,

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:25.870
they were renting them.

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:28.980
I became a guy that actually had my own place.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:34.550
And as crazy as this sounds, Roger Corman
was a little bit of that motivation and it

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:39.070
was also my desire to create a place where
that creativity could grow, where you could

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:44.340
sit outside and have a cigarette or lunch
and sit down next to a writer or sit down

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:52.470
next to a DP or really take a position of
collaborating and that was fun.

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:55.530
Film Courage: Did you have entrepreneurs in
your family?

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:56.980
Did you see them model this?

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:58.560
Jay Silverman: Not a single entrepreneur.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.980
My dad was a sheet Metalist and my mom was
a PTA housewife.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:13.340
I have two siblings and no…I just lucked
out and had a passion for what I did and I

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:17.860
was able to sit back and go “How can I take
that and actually make more of it?”

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:24.390
And just to finish your first question which
relates directly to the formation of setting

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:30.900
up a business and going after what you want,
I’m currently on my third film now.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:38.670
And this film equates to being part of that
vision of working on meaningful projects.

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:43.660
In this particular case, this movie is a romantic
comedy.

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:51.540
It’s also a pretty strong statement about
edible food and inedible food, meaning fast

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:52.800
food and farm-to-table.

